I've been brainstorming a way to extend a semi-supervised adaptation setup where I keep a pretrained vision foundation model backbone (parameters \psi) completely frozen and only train lightweight LoRA experts inserted into selected linear projections of the backbone, plus a classifier head C_{\omega}. For an input image x and a selected expert e, the model prediction is z^{e}(x)=C_{\omega}(f_{\psi}(x;\theta^{e})), where f_{\psi}(\cdot;\theta^{e}) is the frozen VFM equipped with LoRA expert e, \theta^{e} are the expert-specific LoRA parameters, and z^{e}(x) \in \mathbb{R}^{L} are the classification logits.

The idea I'm working through is an Alignment Expert that specifically handles medium-confidence unlabeled samples, which in my current setup (called Positive Only) aren't used this way. The thinking is that these medium-confidence samples often lie near decision boundaries, so rather than forcing a hard pseudo-label decision on them, they could instead be used for soft alignment. The Alignment Expert would be trained by matching the strong-view prediction to the detached weak-view probability via:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}}
 \operatorname{KL}
 \big(
 \operatorname{sg}(p_i^w)
 \,\Vert\,
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{a}(u_i^s))
 \big),
\end{equation}
where \operatorname{KL}(\cdot\Vert\cdot) is the Kullback-Leibler divergence and \operatorname{sg}(\cdot) is the stop-gradient operation. The motivation is that this kind of objective would preserve useful uncertainty information rather than over-confidently fitting to ambiguous samples.

My evaluation setup covers varying labeled-sample counts and unlabeled data compositions across several datasets: CIFAR-100 with N4, N25, and N100 labeled samples per class, FOOD-101 with N2, N4, and N10, Semi-Aves under both in-distribution and in+out-of-distribution unlabeled data, and ImageNet with 1% and 10% labeled data.

My current baseline (Positive Only) accuracy results are: CIFAR-100 N4 = 80.57%, CIFAR-100 N25 = 84.48%, CIFAR-100 N100 = 86.6%; FOOD-101 N2 = 87.62%, FOOD-101 N4 = 89.66%, FOOD-101 N10 = 89.96%; Semi-Aves (in-distribution) = 67.52%, Semi-Aves (in+out-of-distribution) = 61.34%; ImageNet 1% = 74.47%, ImageNet 10% = 79.22%.

My question about this setup: after switching from Positive Only (my baseline above) to Positive + Alignment, predict the resulting value for each: CIFAR-100 N4 accuracy (in %); CIFAR-100 N25 accuracy (in %); CIFAR-100 N100 accuracy (in %); FOOD-101 N2 accuracy (in %); FOOD-101 N4 accuracy (in %); FOOD-101 N10 accuracy (in %); Semi-Aves (in-distribution) accuracy (in %); Semi-Aves (in+out-of-distribution) accuracy (in %); ImageNet 1% accuracy (in %); ImageNet 10% accuracy (in %).

I've been brainstorming around a semi-supervised adaptation setup where I keep a vision foundation model backbone completely frozen and only fine-tune three LoRA experts plus the classifier head. The three experts are a Positive Expert, an Alignment Expert, and a Negative Expert, and the whole point of the design (TriNoL) is to route unlabeled samples to different experts based on pseudo-label confidence rather than randomly, and to give each expert its own tailored loss instead of a shared one.

The routing works like this: I use the weakly augmented unlabeled view to estimate pseudo-label confidence. By default the Positive Expert produces the pseudo-labels since it's trained on labeled data and high-confidence unlabeled samples. For each unlabeled sample $u_i$, I compute $p_i^w=\operatorname{softmax}\big(z^{+}(u_i^w)\big)$, where $z^{+}(u_i^w)$ denotes the logits from the Positive Expert. The pseudo-label and its confidence are $\hat{y}_i=\arg\max_{c} p_{i,c}^w$, $q_i=\max_{c} p_{i,c}^w$.

I use two confidence thresholds, $\tau_{+}$ and $\tau_{-}$, with $0 \leq \tau_{-}<\tau_{+}\leq 1$, and divide unlabeled samples into three confidence regions:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{+} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid q_i \geq \tau_{+}\}, \\
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{a} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid \tau_{-} \leq q_i < \tau_{+}\}, \\
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{-} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid q_i < \tau_{-}\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}$ go to the Positive Expert, samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}$ go to the Alignment Expert, and samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}$ go to the Negative Expert. This routing rule doesn't introduce an extra routing network; it just reuses the same confidence signal that's already present in standard SSL pipelines.

For labeled samples, I anchor adaptation with a supervised loss computed via the Positive Expert:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{sup}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_l|}
 \sum_{(x_i,y_i)\in \mathcal{B}_l}
 \operatorname{CE}
 \big(
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{+}(x_i)), y_i
 \big),
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{CE}(\cdot,\cdot)$ is cross-entropy.

For high-confidence unlabeled samples, the Positive Expert learns from hard pseudo-labels:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pos}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}}
 \operatorname{CE}
 \big(
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{+}(u_i^s)), \hat{y}_i
 \big).
\end{equation}

For medium-confidence unlabeled samples, I don't force a hard pseudo-label decision since these often lie near decision boundaries; instead the Alignment Expert matches the strong-view prediction to the detached weak-view probability:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}
 =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}}
 \operatorname{KL}
 \big(
 \operatorname{sg}(p_i^w)
 \,\Vert\,
 \operatorname{softmax}(z^{a}(u_i^s))
 \big),
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{KL}(\cdot\Vert\cdot)$ is the KL divergence and $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$ is stop-gradient.

For low-confidence unlabeled samples, the Negative Expert is discouraged from assigning high probability to the current top pseudo-label rather than treating it as a clean target:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{neg}}
 =
 -\frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}|}
 \sum_{u_i\in \mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}}
 \log
 \Big(
 1 -
 p_{i,\hat{y}_i}^{-}
 +
 \epsilon
 \Big),
\end{equation}
where $p_i^{-}=\operatorname{softmax}(z^{-}(u_i^s))$, $p_{i,\hat{y}_i}^{-}$ is the probability the Negative Expert assigns to the weak-view top pseudo-label, and $\epsilon$ is a small constant for numerical stability.

The final training objective combines all of these:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}
 =
 \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{sup}}
 +
 \lambda_{\mathrm{pos}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pos}}
 +
 \lambda_{\mathrm{align}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{align}}
 +
 \lambda_{\mathrm{neg}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{neg}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\mathrm{pos}}$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{align}}$, and $\lambda_{\mathrm{neg}}$ control the strengths of the three unlabeled objectives. In practice $\lambda_{\mathrm{pos}}$ is usually larger since high-confidence pseudo-labels give the most direct supervision, the alignment weight controls how much medium-confidence samples affect the decision boundary, and the negative weight is kept moderate to avoid over-penalizing uncertain samples. Only the LoRA experts and classifier head are updated; the VFM backbone stays frozen throughout.

For my routing ablation, I'm comparing this confidence-based routing scheme against two alternatives: random routing, which assigns unlabeled samples to experts randomly, and shared CE, which uses confidence routing but trains all experts with the same hard pseudo-label loss instead of the expert-specific objectives above. I report mean accuracy over 3 trials where available.

My current baseline results with random routing are: CIFAR-100 N4 accuracy = 80.51%, FOOD-101 N2 accuracy = 87.46%, Semi-Aves accuracy = 61.25%, and ImageNet 1% accuracy = 74.43%.

Note that the random-routing arm retains the expert-specific objectives, while the confidence-routing arm here uses a single shared loss, so two factors differ between the arms.

My question about this setup: after switching from Random Routing (my baseline above) to Confidence Routing + Shared CE, predict the resulting value for each: CIFAR-100 N4 accuracy (in %); FOOD-101 N2 accuracy (in %); Semi-Aves accuracy (in %); ImageNet 1% accuracy (in %).

I've been brainstorming around the routing mechanism in my TriNoL setup, where a frozen vision foundation model backbone (backbone parameters denoted by $\psi$, kept frozen during training) has a lightweight LoRA module inserted into selected linear projections, with three LoRA experts as the trainable parameters. The routing uses two confidence thresholds, $\tau_{+}$ and $\tau_{-}$, where $0 \leq \tau_{-}<\tau_{+}\leq 1$, and unlabeled samples are divided into three confidence regions:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{+} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid q_i \geq \tau_{+}\}, \\
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{a} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid \tau_{-} \leq q_i < \tau_{+}\}, \\
 \mathcal{B}_{u}^{-} &= \{u_i \in \mathcal{B}_u \mid q_i < \tau_{-}\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{+}$ are routed to the Positive Expert, samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{a}$ are routed to the Alignment Expert, and samples in $\mathcal{B}_{u}^{-}$ are routed to the Negative Expert. This routing rule is simple and does not introduce an extra routing network. My motivation for looking specifically at $\tau_{+}$ sensitivity is that when $\tau_{+}$ is too low, more unreliable pseudo-labels get routed to the Positive Expert, which weakens the reliable adaptation path, while when $\tau_{+}$ is too high, many useful pseudo-labels are moved away from the Positive Expert, reducing effective supervision. My setup evaluates this on low-label-count regimes, specifically FOOD-101 N2 and Semi-Aves, measuring accuracy. My current baseline results, at $(\tau_{-},\tau_{+})=(0.3,0.7)$, are 88.42% accuracy on FOOD-101 N2 and 61.82% accuracy on Semi-Aves.

Note that this comparison varies the lower threshold tau_- from 0.3 to 0.2 while holding tau_+ fixed at 0.7.

My question about this setup: after switching from (tau_-, tau_+) = (0.3, 0.7) (my baseline above) to (tau_-, tau_+) = (0.2, 0.7), lowering tau_-, predict the resulting value for each: FOOD-101 N2 accuracy (in %); Semi-Aves accuracy (in %).

I've been brainstorming ways to refine the closed-form DCF used inside the QAM module of my EgoHieraLoc VQL-2D pipeline, which operates frame-by-frame on Ego4D-VQ2D. The idea is to add an elliptical deformable constraint to the distance metric that QAM uses when matching candidate peaks across frames. My reasoning is that apparent object shape changes across frames are mostly due to perspective scaling rather than in-plane rotation, so an anisotropic spatial prior aligned with the principal axes of an ellipse should be a reasonable simplification of true deformation.

Concretely, my distance metric combines Euclidean proximity with an elliptical shape constraint:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:distance_metric}
\text{Dis}_{i}^{\text{mod},(v_i)} = \|i' - p_{\text{can}}^{(v_i)}\|_2 + \lambda_e \cdot
\sqrt{\frac{(x_i - p_x)^2}{(a^{(v_i)})^2} + \frac{(y_i - p_y)^2}{(b^{(v_i)})^2}},
\end{equation}
where $(p_x, p_y)$ denotes the coordinates of the candidate peak $p_{\text{can}}^{(v_i)}$, and $\lambda_e = 0.5$ weights the elliptical constraint. The first term enforces proximity to the motion-compensated candidate, while the second term applies the anisotropic spatial prior that penalizes deviations along the principal axes of the ellipse.

To probe the contribution of this component, my setup is an ablation on Ego4D-VQ2D where I evaluate QAM without the elliptical model constraint, comparing it against the full QAM (which also includes multi-peak detection as a separate component I ablate independently). My baseline result for QAM without the elliptical model constraint gives a Success rate (Succ.) of 60.39% on Ego4D-VQ2D.

My question about this setup: after switching from QAM without elliptical model constraint (my baseline above) to Full QAM (with elliptical constraint), predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ2D Success rate (Succ.) (in %).

I've been brainstorming around the hierarchical Transformer backbone used in the RAM (Region Alignment/Aggregation Module, as part of EgoHieraLoc), where hierarchical features $\mathcal{F}_i\in \{\mathcal{F}_1,\mathcal{F}_2,\mathcal{F}_3, \mathcal{F}_4\}$ at four different scales are generated through four stages, each with a similar architectural design. Initially, we transform the dimensions of the features from the video frames (the search region). In the first stage, a $1 \times 1$ convolution block reduces the feature channel dimension from $C$ to $D_s$, constructing embeddings $\mathcal{F}_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{(T \times H_s \times W_s) \times D_s}$. Each subsequent stage takes the feature embeddings from the previous stage as input, generating features $\mathcal{F}_2, \mathcal{F}_3$, and $\mathcal{F}_4$, corresponding to progressively larger strides relative to the input, i.e., a hierarchical downsampling strategy. Denoting the patch size at stage $i$ as $P_i$, at the beginning of stage $i$ the input feature $\mathcal{F}_{i-1} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H_{i-1} \times W_{i-1} \times C_{i-1}}$ is evenly divided into $N_P = \frac{T \times H_{i-1} \times W_{i-1}}{P_i^2}$ patches. Each patch is flattened into a vector of dimension $P_i^2 C_{i-1}$ and projected into an embedding space of $C_i$ dimensions. After the linear projection layer, the shape of the embedded patches can be represented as $(T \times \frac{H_{i-1}}{P_i} \times \frac{W_{i-1}}{P_i}) \times C_{i}$, where height and width are downsampled by a factor of $P_i$ compared to the input. This is formalized as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal{F}_i&=\ell_{i-1}(\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_{i-1} \to \mathbb{R}^{\frac{TH_{i-1}W_{i-1}}{P_i^2} \times P_i^2C_{i-1}})),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\ell_{(\cdot)}$ denotes the linear embedding layer. The feature embedding $\mathcal{F}_i\in \mathbb{R}^{(T \times H_i\times W_i) \times C_i}$ then requires processing through a multi-layer encoder. We constructed a Transformer encoder consisting of $L$ layers, including both attention and feedforward layers. Following prior PVT-style design, the Dimensional Collapse Module (DCM) replaces the traditional Multi-Head Attention (MHA) layer in the encoder to reduce computational costs. For a specific feature embedding $\mathcal{F}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H_i \times W_i \times C_i}$, we first project it into the query space $Q_i \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H_i \times W_i \times C_i}$. For the key and value spaces, we apply a reduction rate denoted as $\varphi_i$, representing their reduction degree at stage $i$, as:
\begin{equation}
K_i=\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_i,\varphi_i)W_K\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{TH_iW_i}{\varphi_i^2}\times C_i},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
V_i=\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_i,\varphi_i)W_V\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{TH_iW_i}{\varphi_i^2}\times C_i},
\end{equation}
where $\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_i, \varphi_i)$ reshapes the input $\mathcal{F}_i$ into a sequence of size $\frac{TH_iW_i}{\varphi_i^2}\times(\varphi_i^2C_i)$, and $W_{(\cdot)}\in\mathbb{R}^{(\varphi_i^2C_i)\times C_i}$ is a linear projection operation that reduces the input sequence's dimensionality to $C_i$. Finally, the multi-scale features from the hierarchical Transformer are integrated into the RAM to achieve feature fusion and refinement.

My experiment setup is an ablation on the RAM within EgoHieraLoc, evaluated on the Ego4D-VQ2D validation set, where I analyze the functional role of the hierarchical Transformer backbone by comparing variants: (i) W/O DCM: a standard Transformer lacking hierarchy without DCM, and (ii) Stage[3,4]: two-stage vs four-stage hierarchical Transformers, i.e., varying the number of feature stages.

My current baseline, with Feature Stage set to Stage 3, gives on the Ego4D-VQ2D validation set: tAP25 = 0.42 score, stAP25 = 0.33 score, Rec.(%) = 50.03%, Succ.(%) = 60.11%.

My question about this setup: after switching from Feature Stage: Stage 3 (my baseline above) to Full Model (Stage 4, w/ DCM, FFN, PE, PVT), predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ2D validation set tAP25 (in score); Ego4D-VQ2D validation set stAP25 (in score); Ego4D-VQ2D validation set Rec.(%) (in %); Ego4D-VQ2D validation set Succ.(%) (in %).

I've been brainstorming about how positional encoding factors into the RAM (hierarchical Transformer) backbone I'm using within EgoHieraLoc. The RAM builds hierarchical features $\mathcal{F}_i\in \{\mathcal{F}_1,\mathcal{F}_2,\mathcal{F}_3, \mathcal{F}_4\}$ at four different scales through four stages, each with a similar architectural design. In the first stage, a $1 \times 1$ convolution block reduces the feature channel dimension from $C$ to $D_s$, constructing embeddings $\mathcal{F}_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{(T \times H_s \times W_s) \times D_s}$. Each subsequent stage takes the previous stage's feature embeddings as input, generating $\mathcal{F}_2, \mathcal{F}_3$, and $\mathcal{F}_4$ at progressively larger strides, following a hierarchical downsampling strategy. Denoting the patch size at stage $i$ as $P_i$, the input feature $\mathcal{F}_{i-1} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H_{i-1} \times W_{i-1} \times C_{i-1}}$ at the start of stage $i$ is evenly divided into $N_P = \frac{T \times H_{i-1} \times W_{i-1}}{P_i^2}$ patches. Each patch is flattened into a vector of dimension $P_i^2 C_{i-1}$ and projected into an embedding space of $C_i$ dimensions, giving embedded patches of shape $(T \times \frac{H_{i-1}}{P_i} \times \frac{W_{i-1}}{P_i}) \times C_{i}$, where height and width are downsampled by a factor of $P_i$ relative to the input. This is formalized as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal{F}_i&=\ell_{i-1}(\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_{i-1} \to \mathbb{R}^{\frac{TH_{i-1}W_{i-1}}{P_i^2} \times P_i^2C_{i-1}})),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\ell_{(\cdot)}$ denotes the linear embedding layer. The feature embedding $\mathcal{F}_i\in \mathbb{R}^{(T \times H_i\times W_i) \times C_i}$ then passes through a multi-layer Transformer encoder of $L$ layers, containing attention and feedforward layers. I use a Dimensional Collapse Module (DCM) in place of the traditional Multi-Head Attention layer in the encoder to reduce computational costs: for a feature embedding $\mathcal{F}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H_i \times W_i \times C_i}$, I first project it into the query space $Q_i \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times H_i \times W_i \times C_i}$, and for the key and value spaces I apply a reduction rate $\varphi_i$ at stage $i$:
\begin{equation}
K_i=\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_i,\varphi_i)W_K\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{TH_iW_i}{\varphi_i^2}\times C_i},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
V_i=\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_i,\varphi_i)W_V\in\mathbb{R}^{\frac{TH_iW_i}{\varphi_i^2}\times C_i},
\end{equation}
where $\text{Reshape}(\mathcal{F}_i, \varphi_i)$ reshapes $\mathcal{F}_i$ into a sequence of size $\frac{TH_iW_i}{\varphi_i^2}\times(\varphi_i^2C_i)$, and $W_{(\cdot)}\in\mathbb{R}^{(\varphi_i^2C_i)\times C_i}$ is a linear projection that reduces the sequence's dimensionality to $C_i$. The multi-scale features from this hierarchical Transformer are then integrated into the RAM for feature fusion and refinement.

As part of my ablation studies analyzing the functional role of this hierarchical Transformer backbone, I set up several variants: (i) W/O DCM: a standard Transformer lacking hierarchy without DCM, (ii) Stage[3,4]: two/four-stage hierarchical Transformers, (iii) W/O FFN: a lightweight variant removing FFN layers for reduced complexity, (iv) W/O PE: a variant without positional encoding, and (vi) a ResNet-101 variant replacing the hierarchical Transformer architecture entirely. My reasoning for testing W/O PE specifically is that, considering object dynamics induced by camera motion in egocentric video, absolute positional encoding may not be the most suitable choice.

My current baseline, evaluated on the Ego4D-VQ2D validation set for the W/O PE variant, gives: tAP25 = 0.43, stAP25 = 0.34, Rec.(%) = 51.04%, and Succ.(%) = 61.18%.

My question about this setup: after switching from w/o PE (my baseline above) to Full Model (Stage 4, w/ DCM, FFN, PE, PVT), predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ2D validation set tAP25; Ego4D-VQ2D validation set stAP25; Ego4D-VQ2D validation set Rec.(%) (in %); Ego4D-VQ2D validation set Succ.(%) (in %).

I've been brainstorming ways to refine the geometric-semantic joint confidence (GSJC) multi-view aggregation scheme I'm using for VQ3D, specifically around how the semantic confidence score is constructed and which of its components actually matter in the aggregation weighting.

Right now the semantic confidence score is built from three probability statistics computed over a segmentation mask $\mathcal{SM}_i$:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{SM}_i|}\sum_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\substack{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i \\ \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i > \wp}} \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}} = \max_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i,
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the count of pixels exceeding threshold $\wp=0.5$, and the three terms are meant to measure mask coverage, high-confidence concentration, and peak certainty respectively. These get combined into a unified semantic score:
\begin{equation}
 \vartheta_i^{\text{sem}} = \frac{1}{3}(\mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}),
\end{equation}
with equal contribution assigned to each component. The idea I'm exploring is around the role of $\mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}}$ specifically as a semantic proxy within this GSJC-based multi-view aggregation.

My experimental setup is an ablation study on the multi-view aggregation function (the GSJC scheme) evaluated on the Ego4D-VQ3D validation set, where the semantic weight $\vartheta^{\text{sem}}$ and geometric weight $\varpi$ are progressively integrated into the aggregation to see how each component's inclusion affects the metrics.

My current baseline, without any aggregation applied, gives: Succ. = 78.13%, Succ.* = 94.32%, L2 = 1.66m, Ang. = 0.76 rad, and QwP(%) = 84.48%, on the Ego4D-VQ3D validation set.

My question about this setup: after switching from Baseline (w/o Aggregation) (my baseline above) to Single Semantic Component with P_thr only, predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ. (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ.* (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set L2 (in m); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Ang. (in rad); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set QwP(%) (in %).

I've been brainstorming about how to improve multi-view aggregation in GSJC by making better use of segmentation mask confidence when combining observations across views. The idea centers on a semantic confidence score for each mask $\mathcal{SM}_i$, built from three probability statistics that jointly characterize mask quality:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{SM}_i|}\sum_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\substack{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i \\ \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i > \wp}} \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}} = \max_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i,
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the count of pixels exceeding threshold $\wp=0.5$, and the three terms measure mask coverage, high-confidence concentration, and peak certainty respectively. These are combined into a unified semantic score:
\begin{equation}
 \vartheta_i^{\text{sem}} = \frac{1}{3}(\mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}),
\end{equation}
assigning equal contribution to each component. My current focus is on isolating the role of $\mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}$ as a semantic proxy within this weighting scheme, since it captures peak-response information about a mask that the average and threshold-based terms don't directly reflect.

My setup is an ablation on the Ego4D-VQ3D validation set, evaluating the impact of this semantic weighting on GSJC-based multi-view aggregation. I'm tracking Succ., Succ.*, L2, Ang., and QwP(%) as metrics. The baseline, without any aggregation applied, gives: Succ. = 78.13%, Succ.* = 94.32%, L2 = 1.66m, Ang. = 0.76 rad, and QwP(%) = 84.48%. From here I'm examining how incorporating $\mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}$ specifically, either alongside the other proxies or as an individual component, affects these numbers relative to this baseline.

My question about this setup: after switching from Baseline (w/o Aggregation) (my baseline above) to Single Semantic Component: P_max, predict the resulting value for each: Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ. (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Succ.* (in %); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set L2 (in m); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set Ang. (in rad); Ego4D-VQ3D validation set QwP(%) (in %).

I've been brainstorming ways to make the multi-view aggregation step in my GSJC scheme more robust by leveraging combined semantic proxies rather than a single confidence measure. The setup works like this: for each segmentation mask $\mathcal{SM}_i$, I compute three probability statistics that together characterize mask quality:
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{SM}_i|}\sum_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{\substack{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i \\ \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i > \wp}} \text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i, \quad \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}} = \max_{(x,y)\in \mathcal{SM}_i}\text{pr}_{(x,y)}^i,
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the count of pixels exceeding threshold $\wp=0.5$, and the three terms measure mask coverage, high-confidence concentration, and peak certainty respectively. Normally I combine all three into a unified semantic score:
\begin{equation}
 \vartheta_i^{\text{sem}} = \frac{1}{3}(\mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{thr}} + \mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}),
\end{equation}
giving equal contribution to each component. For this particular ablation, though, I'm looking specifically at the combined effect of just two of those semantic proxies, $\mathcal{P}_{\text{ave}}$ and $\mathcal{P}_{\text{max}}$ (P_ave+P_max), within the broader multi-view aggregation pipeline.

That pipeline combines all confidence factors multiplicatively into a composite weight:
\begin{equation}
 \tilde{\vartheta}_i = \max\left(\vartheta_i^{\text{sem}} \cdot \varpi_i^{\text{depth}} \cdot \varpi_i^{\text{reproj}} \cdot \varpi_i^{\text{tri}},\; \epsilon_w\right),
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon_w = 10^{-6}$ prevents numerical underflow, ensuring that any significantly unreliable factor suppresses that frame's contribution while frames robust across all dimensions receive full weight. The final 3D position is then a confidence-weighted mean:
\begin{equation}
 [\hat{x}, \hat{y}, \hat{z}]^T = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{N_C} \tilde{\vartheta}_j \cdot [x_j, y_j, z_j]^T}{\sum_{j=1}^{N_C} \tilde{\vartheta}_j}.
\end{equation}
When all frames are severely unreliable and all weights clamp to $\epsilon_w$, the denominator approaches $N_C \epsilon_w$ and the aggregation gracefully defaults to an unweighted average, which keeps things numerically stable. Finally, I transform the aggregated world position into the query frame's coordinate system via the inverse query pose:
\begin{equation}
 \boldsymbol{\delta} = \mathcal{T}_{\text{query}}^{-1}[\hat{x}, \hat{y}, \hat{z}, 1]^T,
\end{equation}
yielding the VQL-3D prediction.

I'm running this as part of a GSJC-based multi-view aggregation ablation on the Ego4D-VQ3D validation set, evaluating Succ., Succ.*, L2, Ang., and QwP(%). My baseline, without any aggregation, currently gets: Succ. = 78.13%, Succ.* = 94.32%, L2 = 1.66m, Ang. = 0.76 rad, and QwP(%) = 84.48%.

