I've been working on a two-stage pipeline for leukemia cell classification, where Stage 2 performs subtype classification (ALL vs. AML), applied conditionally to the samples predicted as leukemic by Stage 1. For this stage I'm testing three encoders — DinoBloom, BiomedCLIP, and CLIP — cross-dataset trained on 69,400 single-cell images, and evaluated using a held-out dataset protocol on ALL-IDB2 + AML-Cytomorphology (n=3424).

One idea I've been brainstorming to improve this stage is Retrieval-Augmented Classification (RAC). The general concept behind retrieval-augmented methods in AI is to give a pretrained model an external memory retrieval mechanism so it can fetch task-specific information and enhance performance. For VFM-based image classifiers, this means the model doesn't only rely on its pretrained visual representations, but also incorporates additional context to help classify an image more accurately — this is what I'm calling RAC.

The mechanism I've set up consists of a parallel retrieval branch combined with the image encoder. At inference time, the retrieval branch queries pre-encoded images, which provide additional context and are fused with the base classification to create one final classification. Specifically, the RAC module works on the embedding space produced by the frozen or LoRA-adapted encoder. Given a query image, its embedding is compared by cosine similarity against an embedding bank comprising the L2-normalized training embeddings produced by the encoder. The top-$k$ most similar samples make a similarity-weighted vote over classes, forming a retrieval distribution $p_{\text{ret}}$. This distribution is then fused with the linear classifier distribution $p_{\text{probe}}$ in log space:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rac}
\text{logits}_{\text{final}} = \log p_{\text{probe}} + \alpha \log p_{\text{ret}}
\end{equation}

where $\alpha$ weights the contribution of the retrieval branch. For the linear probe configurations, $\alpha$ was selected on an internal validation split derived from the training datasets, searching over the grid $\{0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1\}$. For the LoRA configurations, $\alpha$ was fixed at $0.5$ due to computational budget. The held-out datasets were never used for tuning. The predicted class corresponds to the highest resulting logit, and $k$ was set to 20 in all experiments.

My current baseline, before applying RAC, on the held-out set (ALL-IDB2 + AML-Cytomorphology, n=3424) is as follows, using Linear probe / LoRA configurations without RAC: overall Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903. Broken down by encoder configuration: DinoBloom LoRA gives Accuracy = 0.9617, Recall = 0.4998, F1-score = 0.4902. BiomedCLIP Linear probe gives Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903. BiomedCLIP LoRA gives Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903. CLIP Linear probe gives Accuracy = 0.9118, Recall = 0.485, F1-score = 0.4867. CLIP LoRA gives Accuracy = 0.962, Recall = 0.5, F1-score = 0.4903.

My question about this setup: after switching from Linear probe / LoRA (without RAC) (my baseline above) to Linear probe + RAC / LoRA + RAC, predict the resulting value for each: Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom linear probe Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom linear probe Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom linear probe F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom LoRA Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom LoRA Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, DinoBloom LoRA F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP linear probe Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP linear probe Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP linear probe F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP LoRA Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP LoRA Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, BiomedCLIP LoRA F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP linear probe Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP linear probe Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP linear probe F1-score (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP LoRA Accuracy (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP LoRA Recall (in proportion); Stage 2 held-out, CLIP LoRA F1-score (in proportion).

I've been spending cycles thinking about how the number of Monte Carlo samples $N$ used in the TD target estimation interacts with the number of forward simulation steps $M$ in PI-VM. The target value $\hat{V}_{\theta}$ is estimated via a Monte Carlo approximation of the path integral, where given $N$ sample trajectories, the target is computed as:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:target_value}
\begin{aligned}
    \hat{V}_{\theta}(x, t, s) = -\log \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=1}^N \exp\left( - \hat{\mathcal{W}}(X^{(j)}_{[t,s]}) - \hat{\mathcal{G}}(X^{(j)}_{s}) \right) ,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where each trajectory contributes to this estimate, and I wanted to understand how varying $N$ jointly with $M$ affects both control accuracy and computational cost. My setup uses PI-VM trained with the same network and training configuration as in the main experiments, and I've set up an ablation grid where $N$ (sample size) and $M$ (forward steps) are jointly varied. For each combination in the grid, I record two values: the control $\mathcal{L}_2$ error (top value) and the runtime (bottom value). My baseline in this ablation grid, at $N=4, M=8$, gets a control $\mathcal{L}_2$ error of 0.00011 and a runtime of 7.0ms.

My question about this setup: after switching from N=4, M=8 (my baseline above) to N=16, M=8, predict the resulting value for each: PI-VM ablation grid at M=8, varying sample size N Control L2 Error; PI-VM ablation grid at M=8, varying sample size N Runtime (in ms).

I've been brainstorming about how the token-budget schedule across depth should be determined in TAP-F, which is built on a frozen ViT-B backbone with task-specific adapters. Right now my setup allocates the retained token budget per layer using a learned readout: the target keep rate defines $K=\max\{1,\operatorname{round}(\rho N_1)\}$ and $R=N_1-K$, with rounding implemented as $\operatorname{round}(x)=\lfloor x+1/2\rfloor$ for nonnegative $x$ to avoid dependence on a library's tie convention. Before any removal, $B^{(l_1)}=R$. At a non-final pruning layer, a readout $\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^d$ and $b\in\mathbb{R}$ proposes a fraction of the unspent budget, with capacity clipping to prevent that layer from removing every remaining patch:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
g^{(l)} &= \mathbf{w}^{\top}\mathbf{r}_t^{(l)}+b, \\
\widetilde R^{(l)} &= \sigma(g^{(l)})B^{(l)}, \\
C^{(l)} &= \min\{B^{(l)},N_l-1\}, \\
R^{(l)} &= \min\{\operatorname{round}(\widetilde R^{(l)}),C^{(l)}\}, \quad l\ne l_M, \\
B^{(l^+)} &= B^{(l)}-R^{(l)}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:supp-budget}
\end{equation}
The final pruning layer sets $R^{(l_M)}=B^{(l_M)}$, which removes the rounding residue and produces the exact global count. The allocation and recovery readouts have no task-indexed branches and are reused across layers; task dependence enters through the active register. TAP-J learns these components within each separately trained task checkpoint, while TAP-F shares one allocation readout across all tasks and one recovery readout across the dense tasks. Each forward pass contains exactly one active register, selected by the task identifier before the image enters the backbone, and switching tasks replaces that register and, in TAP-F, activates the corresponding low-rank updates and task head while the frozen backbone remains shared. The shared readouts receive the active register state and produce decisions that depend on both the task and the current image. In my ablations, each change fixes the backbone, adapters, token count, training budget, and seeds while varying one component. Register controls separate task identity from feature adaptation because a shared initial register may still receive task-specific adapter features. Allocation controls record every layer count and verify the exact-budget identity for each image. Recovery endpoint controls reuse the same retained sets and pointers. One thing I've been considering is replacing this learned, per-image, per-layer allocation policy with a layer-uniform allocation schedule instead that removes an equal share at every pruning layer (a 33/33/34 split), leaving all other components unchanged. My baseline results at keep rate rho=0.5 with the layer-uniform allocation are: ImageNet-1K top-1 accuracy = 82.0%, ADE20K mIoU = 44.6 mIoU, and COCO box AP = 51.7 box AP.

My question about this setup: after switching from Layer-uniform allocation (my baseline above) to TAP-F full, predict the resulting value for each: ImageNet-1K top-1 accuracy (in %); ADE20K mIoU (in mIoU); COCO box AP (in box AP).

I've been sketching out how to balance the different loss terms in UHDformer++ for the UHD low-light enhancement task. Since the model uses both LHFR and SR to reconstruct the high-resolution images, the overall loss has two parts, L_LHFR and L_SR:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{UHDformer++}}
=&\underbrace{\|\mathbf{\hat{H}}-\mathbf{H}\|_1
+\lambda_{freq}\|\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{\hat{H}})-\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{H})\|_1}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{LHFR}}} \\
&+\underbrace{\lambda_{re\_sr}\|\mathbf{\hat{H}}-\mathbf{H}\|_1
+\lambda_{freq\_sr}\|\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{\hat{H}})-\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{H})\|_1}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SR}}},
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:loss_uhdformerpp}
\end{equation}

where F denotes the Fast Fourier transform. Each loss constrains the restored results H_hat against the ground truth images H, combining an image reconstruction loss for pixel recovery with a frequency loss for detail enhancement. In my setup, the loss weights are empirically set to lambda_freq=0.05, lambda_re_sr=0.5, and lambda_freq_sr=1, and I'm specifically running a sweep over lambda_re_sr, the weight of the reconstruction loss in SR space, to analyze its contribution alongside the L1 and frequency loss terms applied in both the reconstruction and SR spaces. My baseline, with lambda_re_sr = 0.5 (the default), gets on UHD-LL: PSNR = 27.256dB, SSIM = 0.9308, and LPIPS = 0.2304.

My question about this setup: after switching from lambda_re_sr = 0.5 (Default) (my baseline above) to lambda_re_sr = 0, predict the resulting value for each: UHD-LL PSNR (dB) (in dB); UHD-LL SSIM; UHD-LL LPIPS.
